WEBVTT

00:00.000 --> 00:10.760
All right, so today we're going to continue our discussion about trajectory planning

00:10.760 --> 00:13.200
and trajectory optimization.

00:13.200 --> 00:17.940
So last time we formulated the trajectory optimization problem.

00:17.940 --> 00:23.280
We discussed at a high level what are the possible methodologies out there to tackle

00:23.280 --> 00:25.780
the trajectory optimization problem.

00:25.780 --> 00:34.620
In particular, we discussed two main classes of approaches, direct methods and indirect

00:34.620 --> 00:35.620
methods.

00:35.620 --> 00:42.300
They try to compute a feasible trajectory that on top of that optimizes some cost function

00:42.300 --> 00:45.740
by using different philosophies.

00:45.740 --> 00:52.420
The topic of indirect methods is a bit more advanced than this class that is covered in

00:52.420 --> 00:53.860
a class that I teach in the spring.

00:54.300 --> 00:56.200
I'll just give you the high level ideas.

00:56.200 --> 00:58.100
And on Friday, meaning tomorrow,

00:58.100 --> 01:00.700
I will go a little bit deeper into this topic.

01:01.960 --> 01:06.700
But what we did was to say, well, many times

01:06.700 --> 01:09.380
we don't really want a necessary and optimal solution.

01:09.380 --> 01:11.700
A feasible solution that is good enough

01:11.700 --> 01:14.220
as long as I can compute it quickly

01:14.220 --> 01:17.540
will be sufficient for the purposes of trajectory planning.

01:17.540 --> 01:19.420
And we found out that there is a technique

01:19.420 --> 01:21.260
referred to as differential flatness

01:21.260 --> 01:23.660
that allow us to do precisely that.

01:23.660 --> 01:26.060
For a specific class of systems that have the property

01:26.060 --> 01:28.820
of differential flatness, it is possible to compute

01:28.820 --> 01:30.780
a good enough trajectory very quickly.

01:30.780 --> 01:34.540
Indeed, to turn the problem of computing

01:34.540 --> 01:38.140
a feasible trajectory into an algebraic problem

01:38.140 --> 01:43.020
to compute the same coefficients of parameterization

01:43.020 --> 01:44.260
of a feasible trajectory.

01:45.420 --> 01:48.460
So today, we are going to continue this discussion

01:48.460 --> 01:50.340
along three main lines.

01:50.340 --> 01:53.460
First of all, we're going to talk a little bit more

01:54.100 --> 01:57.300
in detail about how to account for constraints

01:57.300 --> 01:59.700
in the context of differential flatness.

01:59.700 --> 02:01.380
If you remember last time,

02:01.380 --> 02:04.220
we just computed a trajectory,

02:04.220 --> 02:07.340
but we did not explicitly enforce any constraints

02:07.340 --> 02:08.700
in the flat outer space.

02:08.700 --> 02:13.020
With a consequence that in the real state space,

02:13.020 --> 02:14.780
we might have a trajectory that violates

02:14.780 --> 02:15.860
the sum of the constraints.

02:15.860 --> 02:20.020
So the question is, how do we encode constraints,

02:20.020 --> 02:22.540
in particular input bound constraints

02:22.540 --> 02:24.340
in differential flatness context?

02:25.340 --> 02:28.220
The second topic will be, okay, now we have a way

02:28.220 --> 02:30.100
to compute an open loop trajectory.

02:30.100 --> 02:33.260
We go back to the two-step design that we discussed

02:33.260 --> 02:34.180
last time.

02:34.180 --> 02:37.180
How do we augment an open loop trajectory

02:37.180 --> 02:41.380
with a tacking controller that imbues some robustness

02:41.380 --> 02:42.980
in the overall control scheme?

02:44.500 --> 02:49.500
Finally, we're going to discuss closed loop control.

02:50.500 --> 02:52.300
And in particular, we're going to discuss

02:52.300 --> 02:54.540
a specific application of closed loop control

02:54.540 --> 02:58.740
to posture stabilization that is going to be used,

02:58.740 --> 03:03.140
as we will see, in conjunction with the two-step design

03:03.140 --> 03:06.860
in order to have an end-to-end trajectory planning module

03:06.860 --> 03:08.980
for your differential drive robots

03:08.980 --> 03:11.220
that is robust and efficient.

03:11.220 --> 03:13.020
So these are the three main topics

03:13.020 --> 03:15.100
that we're going to cover today.

03:15.100 --> 03:18.780
Before we do so, I want just to provide a summary,

03:19.140 --> 03:21.500
a more detailed summary of what we did last time.

03:21.500 --> 03:24.180
As I said, last time we focused on the concept

03:24.180 --> 03:27.700
of differential flatness to compute a feasible trajectory.

03:30.420 --> 03:32.620
In particular, the problem of differential,

03:32.620 --> 03:36.860
the property of differential flatness reads as follows.

03:36.860 --> 03:41.860
In the linear system described by ordinary differential

03:42.020 --> 03:45.620
equation, X dot equal to AXU, X is the state,

03:45.660 --> 03:49.100
U is the input, is differentially flat.

03:49.100 --> 03:52.260
If there exists a set of output C,

03:52.260 --> 03:57.220
the function of X, U, and possibly some derivatives of U,

03:57.220 --> 04:01.060
with a property that the knowledge of the flat output C

04:01.060 --> 04:03.740
and its derivative up to order Q

04:03.740 --> 04:07.220
allow us to backtrack what is the set of states,

04:07.220 --> 04:11.660
and most importantly, the set of inputs that provide

04:16.220 --> 04:17.940
the trajectory Z.

04:17.940 --> 04:20.980
Basically, this allows us to find a one-to-one mapping

04:20.980 --> 04:25.500
between trajectories, pairs of states, and controls X, U,

04:25.500 --> 04:28.300
and the flat outputs for which the dynamics

04:28.300 --> 04:31.100
have effectively been canceled.

04:34.180 --> 04:35.420
Any questions about this?

04:38.740 --> 04:41.140
So from intuition standpoint, differential flatness

04:41.140 --> 04:45.380
captures the notion of whether a curve

04:45.380 --> 04:49.700
in the flat output space can be realized

04:49.700 --> 04:52.780
in the original X space through an appropriate

04:52.780 --> 04:55.780
selection of inputs, with the key idea that

04:55.780 --> 04:59.420
if that is the case, it's much easier to compute a curve

04:59.420 --> 05:01.780
as opposed to compute the solution

05:01.780 --> 05:03.140
to a differential equation.

05:06.020 --> 05:06.860
Any questions?

05:06.860 --> 05:08.300
So this is pretty fundamental, so I want to make sure

05:08.300 --> 05:10.020
that everyone is on the same page.

05:11.260 --> 05:16.260
All right, so if the system is differentially flat,

05:17.180 --> 05:22.180
and as we said, not all systems are differentially flat,

05:22.740 --> 05:25.500
but fortunately, for the sake of robotics,

05:25.500 --> 05:28.260
a number of systems that are of interest in practice

05:28.260 --> 05:29.980
are differentially flat.

05:29.980 --> 05:34.940
If that is the case, it's possible to plan a curve

05:34.940 --> 05:39.940
in a flat output space, Z, and then through the mappings

05:39.980 --> 05:42.300
beta and gamma, and in particular gamma,

05:42.300 --> 05:45.820
to recover the inputs that allow us to realize this,

05:45.820 --> 05:47.980
to translate this trajectory into a trajectory

05:47.980 --> 05:51.180
that is dynamically feasible in the original state space X.

05:52.180 --> 05:54.260
Now in order to compute this curve,

05:54.260 --> 05:58.260
then we can use a variety of interpolation methods.

05:58.260 --> 06:00.620
So the one that we've been discussing last time,

06:00.620 --> 06:02.700
and you're going to use for the first problem

06:02.700 --> 06:07.180
in your p-set, entails polynomial interpolation.

06:07.180 --> 06:12.180
So the idea is that each variable ZJ of T,

06:14.300 --> 06:18.020
ZJ is the jth variable of the vector Z,

06:18.020 --> 06:21.780
or the flat output Z, is going to be represented

06:21.780 --> 06:26.780
as a polynomial, alpha i, j,

06:26.780 --> 06:31.780
times a polynomial basis.

06:39.380 --> 06:42.700
The psi i's provide a polynomial basis,

06:42.700 --> 06:44.260
like for example, as I said last time,

06:44.260 --> 06:49.260
psi one could be one, psi two T could be T,

06:49.580 --> 06:54.580
psi three T could be T squared, and so on.

06:56.860 --> 07:00.180
So basically we parameterize each component

07:00.180 --> 07:03.420
of the flat output as a polynomial.

07:03.420 --> 07:07.180
These coefficients are unknown.

07:07.180 --> 07:11.500
They are computed by enforcing the boundary conditions.

07:11.500 --> 07:13.980
In particular, we have boundary conditions

07:13.980 --> 07:18.260
for the state X, for example, X of T zero

07:18.260 --> 07:22.660
is equal to X zero, X to T F is equal to X F.

07:23.660 --> 07:27.300
So these boundary conditions are translated

07:27.300 --> 07:30.660
into boundary conditions for the flat outputs,

07:30.660 --> 07:33.420
and it allows you to pinpoint the exact values

07:33.420 --> 07:34.340
for the coefficients.

07:34.340 --> 07:37.500
For example, for the first problem, your p-set,

07:37.500 --> 07:41.420
I'm asking you to consider a cubic polynomial interpolation

07:41.420 --> 07:43.220
in order to compute the curve.

07:44.700 --> 07:49.700
You have two flat outputs, X and Y, right?

07:50.500 --> 07:53.660
And in order to completely reconstruct the state,

07:53.660 --> 07:56.580
you also need the derivatives, X dot and Y dot.

07:59.700 --> 08:02.140
And since you have two flat outputs,

08:02.140 --> 08:04.020
you're going to, and you have a cubic interpolation,

08:04.020 --> 08:07.480
you're going to have four coefficients per flat output,

08:07.480 --> 08:10.440
so you have to determine eight coefficients.

08:11.940 --> 08:16.700
But then you have boundary conditions at the beginning

08:16.700 --> 08:20.900
and at the end for each, for both X and Y,

08:20.900 --> 08:23.260
and for its derivative, so you have eight conditions.

08:23.260 --> 08:26.020
That allows you to set up a system of linear equations

08:26.020 --> 08:28.700
that allow you to compute the coefficients.

08:28.700 --> 08:32.240
Now, here we're only talking about feasibility.

08:32.240 --> 08:34.100
If you want to go beyond feasibility,

08:34.100 --> 08:36.140
actually you want to compute the flat output

08:36.140 --> 08:38.160
that has some desirable property,

08:38.160 --> 08:42.640
then what you do is to overparameterize your trajectory,

08:42.640 --> 08:45.400
so basically having more coefficients than constraints,

08:45.400 --> 08:48.220
and then use a cost function to compute the coefficients

08:48.220 --> 08:50.200
that on top of guaranteeing feasibility,

08:50.200 --> 08:52.320
meaning satisfying the boundary conditions,

08:52.320 --> 08:54.040
also optimizes that cost function.

08:54.040 --> 08:56.520
For example, minimization of jerk, a snap.

08:58.120 --> 08:58.960
Is this clear?

09:01.120 --> 09:03.220
So the mathematics at the end of the day

09:03.220 --> 09:04.080
are not too important.

09:04.080 --> 09:05.600
What is very important for you to know

09:05.600 --> 09:07.840
is that for a very important class of system

09:07.840 --> 09:09.600
referred to as differentially flat,

09:09.600 --> 09:11.480
the problem of computing a trajectory

09:11.480 --> 09:15.080
can be converted to the problem of computing a curve

09:15.600 --> 09:17.300
for a subset of the state variables.

09:17.300 --> 09:19.200
Computing a curve can be done very easily

09:19.200 --> 09:21.120
through polynomial interpolation.

09:21.120 --> 09:22.680
Once you have computed that curve,

09:22.680 --> 09:25.940
then by a linear mapping that someone

09:25.940 --> 09:29.460
has already done for you, you can find the inputs

09:29.460 --> 09:32.920
that gives rise to the trajectory in the original space X

09:32.920 --> 09:37.220
that is feasible, and possibly with some optimality property

09:37.220 --> 09:40.120
in terms of minimization of jerk and snap,

09:40.120 --> 09:41.920
which means that if in the future

09:41.920 --> 09:45.320
you have to control a robotic system,

09:45.320 --> 09:47.180
first thing that you should do

09:47.180 --> 09:49.120
is to see if it's differentially flat.

09:49.120 --> 09:50.640
Proving differential flatness is hard,

09:50.640 --> 09:53.540
so what you do is you go on Scholar Google,

09:53.540 --> 09:55.960
you type what your system is,

09:55.960 --> 09:57.800
and hopefully that system falls

10:00.180 --> 10:02.760
in the category of system that is differentially flat.

10:02.760 --> 10:03.600
Great.

10:03.600 --> 10:06.680
If that is the case, you look at what these mappings are,

10:06.680 --> 10:08.200
and then you apply just a procedure

10:08.200 --> 10:10.120
that you have learned in this class.

10:10.160 --> 10:13.120
If it's not differentially flat, then you call me,

10:13.120 --> 10:15.560
and then I give you the lecture slides for two or three,

10:15.560 --> 10:17.400
where I tell you how to basically

10:17.400 --> 10:18.560
do trajectory optimization

10:18.560 --> 10:20.560
if the system is not differentially flat.

10:21.520 --> 10:22.360
Yeah?

10:23.240 --> 10:28.040
The output is almost just a subset of the state variable.

10:28.040 --> 10:30.720
Well, yeah, it's more specifically

10:32.520 --> 10:37.160
a function of the state, and possibly also the input,

10:37.160 --> 10:40.240
and possibly some derivatives of the input.

10:41.560 --> 10:43.080
It's full generality.

10:43.080 --> 10:48.080
For the case of the extended unicycle model,

10:48.440 --> 10:50.220
the one that you have in the problem set,

10:50.220 --> 10:53.800
it turns out that it's just a subset of the state variables,

10:53.800 --> 10:58.040
in particular the Cartesian position of the vehicle, x, y.

10:58.040 --> 11:02.000
But that is not, it just happened to be the case

11:02.000 --> 11:05.240
in that case, but for example in the case of a quadrotor,

11:05.240 --> 11:08.400
you also have yo, apart from the Cartesian position

11:08.400 --> 11:09.900
as an additional flat output.

11:13.060 --> 11:15.900
But in general, it turns out that the flat output,

11:17.980 --> 11:21.200
and the exact reason it's not clear to date, I would argue,

11:21.200 --> 11:24.000
typically have a physical meaning.

11:24.000 --> 11:26.700
So yes, typically is some quantity

11:26.700 --> 11:28.200
that has a physical interpretation,

11:28.200 --> 11:30.520
like x, y position, or heading, and so on.

11:30.520 --> 11:33.800
It's not some weird, from this definition,

11:33.800 --> 11:36.400
the flat output could be some very weird

11:36.400 --> 11:37.820
combination of states, right?

11:37.820 --> 11:39.720
Typically that is not the case.

11:39.720 --> 11:42.040
Typically z is just a subset.

11:44.360 --> 11:46.040
All right.

11:46.040 --> 11:48.440
Now in all this procedure, as I said,

11:48.440 --> 11:50.600
what you do to pinpoint your polynomial

11:50.600 --> 11:53.360
that defines your curve in a flat output space,

11:53.360 --> 11:55.460
we consider boundary constraints.

11:55.460 --> 11:58.860
But these are the only constraints that we account for

11:58.860 --> 12:01.580
when planning a trajectory with differential flatness.

12:01.580 --> 12:03.480
What about additional constraints?

12:04.000 --> 12:06.320
Like, for example, input bound constraints.

12:06.320 --> 12:08.460
The constraint, in the case of a unicycle,

12:08.460 --> 12:10.800
that the driving speed and the angular speed

12:10.800 --> 12:13.420
have to be less than an upper bound.

12:14.520 --> 12:15.880
How do I account for those?

12:16.760 --> 12:21.760
So in general, constraints on x and u

12:21.760 --> 12:24.760
are translated into constraints on the curvature

12:24.760 --> 12:28.520
of the curve on the flat outputs,

12:28.520 --> 12:30.640
on the curve that defines the time history

12:30.640 --> 12:32.080
of the flat output.

12:32.080 --> 12:37.080
The intuition is that if you have sharp corners,

12:37.080 --> 12:40.960
this translates typically in very high control efforts.

12:40.960 --> 12:41.880
That's intuition.

12:42.760 --> 12:47.320
So the procedure to translate general constraints

12:47.320 --> 12:50.880
from the original x, u space into the flat output space

12:50.880 --> 12:52.680
is beyond the scope of this class.

12:52.680 --> 12:55.000
But what I want to do today,

12:55.000 --> 12:57.200
as is the first part of the class,

12:57.200 --> 12:59.920
is to tell you a very effective way

12:59.920 --> 13:02.640
to account, arguably, for the most important class

13:02.640 --> 13:06.080
of constraints, which are the bound constraints,

13:06.080 --> 13:07.240
the input bound constraints.

13:07.240 --> 13:09.500
Typically, for all dynamical systems,

13:09.500 --> 13:12.940
you have an upper bound on much control authority you have.

13:12.940 --> 13:15.300
So what I want to do now is to discuss how,

13:15.300 --> 13:17.080
in the context of differential flatness,

13:17.080 --> 13:19.080
we can account for input bound constraints.

13:19.080 --> 13:23.200
For example, top velocity less than v max,

13:23.200 --> 13:26.900
say, I think it's 0.5 meter per second in your P sets,

13:26.900 --> 13:29.040
omega t less than omega max.

13:29.040 --> 13:29.880
Yeah?

13:31.880 --> 13:33.320
If R is z, for example,

13:33.320 --> 13:36.940
we're trying to also minimize the jerk in the style of x,

13:36.940 --> 13:37.780
then is that-

13:37.780 --> 13:40.540
Oh, that's not, we're reminding the jerk of z.

13:40.540 --> 13:44.100
So when we do the planning problem,

13:44.100 --> 13:47.760
it's entirely in the flat output space.

13:47.760 --> 13:52.760
Turns out that if you typically get

13:53.000 --> 13:55.560
a very smooth curve in the flat output space,

13:55.560 --> 14:00.200
that corresponds to a very nice trajectory in the x space.

14:00.200 --> 14:01.200
But in terms of planning,

14:01.200 --> 14:03.020
and that's the whole point of differential flatness,

14:03.020 --> 14:06.440
you're operating in the flat output space.

14:06.440 --> 14:09.320
That's why we also want to be able to account for constraints

14:09.320 --> 14:10.640
in the flat output space.

14:12.800 --> 14:16.640
So the strategy that I'm going to discuss

14:16.640 --> 14:19.160
is referred to as time scaling.

14:19.160 --> 14:21.360
Extremely useful to account for bound constraints

14:21.360 --> 14:22.320
in the flat output space,

14:22.320 --> 14:27.320
but in general, it's a widely used strategy in robotics

14:27.520 --> 14:31.080
to optimize trajectories for manipulators, for example,

14:31.080 --> 14:33.040
beyond wheeled vehicles.

14:34.040 --> 14:38.920
So the idea of time scaling is easy,

14:38.920 --> 14:43.040
but you should take a little bit of time

14:43.040 --> 14:46.480
to really get familiar to it.

14:46.480 --> 14:48.880
You might get a little bit confusing, basically,

14:48.880 --> 14:51.400
in terms of how you set up the notation and so on.

14:51.400 --> 14:55.240
I'm trying to explain it in the simplest possible way.

14:55.240 --> 14:58.880
The idea of time scaling from an intuitive standpoint

14:58.880 --> 14:59.720
is as follows.

15:07.160 --> 15:11.560
Let's consider again the problem of finding a curve

15:11.560 --> 15:13.720
for the flat outputs, z.

15:14.440 --> 15:19.440
So the curve ZT tells you at each time T

15:22.520 --> 15:24.960
what the value of the flat output should be.

15:25.960 --> 15:28.400
As time changes, then this point changes.

15:29.320 --> 15:32.960
The idea of time scaling is to separate the notion

15:32.960 --> 15:36.240
of a path, which is a purely geometric notion,

15:36.240 --> 15:38.120
basically just this curve,

15:38.120 --> 15:43.120
from the notion of the time scale of the flat output space

15:43.720 --> 15:46.640
and sequence that you use to go along that path.

15:47.920 --> 15:50.400
So basically, the idea is to separate the computation

15:50.400 --> 15:54.640
of the dramatic path from the time schedule

15:54.640 --> 15:58.120
according to which you reach each point along this path.

15:58.120 --> 16:00.000
If you are given a path,

16:00.000 --> 16:02.120
if you are someone that runs very fast,

16:02.120 --> 16:05.200
you may reach this point, say, in one second.

16:05.200 --> 16:09.240
If you are slow, you reach this point in two seconds.

16:09.240 --> 16:13.040
Roughly speaking, if you keep this difference in speed,

16:13.960 --> 16:18.840
the first runner reaches the end condition in time T,

16:19.800 --> 16:23.520
and the second runner reaches the same point in time 2T,

16:23.520 --> 16:25.160
so it takes double the time.

16:25.160 --> 16:27.360
But they have followed exactly the same path.

16:28.960 --> 16:31.080
So the idea of time scaling is to separate

16:31.080 --> 16:32.920
the notion of a geometric path

16:32.920 --> 16:36.480
from the notion of the time schedule that is used

16:36.480 --> 16:38.520
in order to march along that path.

16:40.240 --> 16:42.480
So why is that useful?

16:42.480 --> 16:45.720
I mean, this is useful because then you can just compute

16:45.720 --> 16:50.680
a path neglecting the input bound constraints

16:50.680 --> 16:53.000
that usually relate to how quickly

16:53.000 --> 16:56.760
you want to go through that path from the time schedule

16:56.760 --> 17:00.960
that you can adjust in order to fulfill those bounds.

17:03.840 --> 17:05.480
Does it make sense?

17:05.480 --> 17:08.720
So in particular, the idea from a mathematical standpoint

17:08.720 --> 17:13.360
is to view a function of time T

17:13.360 --> 17:18.360
as a function of T through a time scaling law, S,

17:20.320 --> 17:24.560
where S is a function such that S T zero

17:26.200 --> 17:31.120
is equal to T zero, S T F is equal to T F.

17:31.120 --> 17:35.480
Most importantly, S dot T is greater than zero,

17:35.480 --> 17:39.280
which means that basically when you do the, oh, sorry.

17:44.320 --> 17:46.360
S zero, S F.

17:46.360 --> 17:49.840
Which basically means that as you rescale time,

17:49.840 --> 17:52.680
you keep the property that you cannot go backward in time.

17:53.880 --> 17:56.200
So you need to have this monotonicity property.

17:57.400 --> 18:00.760
So here you see how basically we are separating

18:00.760 --> 18:02.800
the geometric notion of a path

18:02.800 --> 18:05.720
from the time schedule of the path.

18:08.200 --> 18:12.160
So then it's very typical to optimize the geometric path

18:12.160 --> 18:16.920
with respect to arc length, S.

18:16.920 --> 18:18.880
The arc length is the distance

18:18.880 --> 18:20.680
between two points around the path.

18:22.680 --> 18:27.640
And then specify a timing law that tells you

18:27.640 --> 18:29.640
how you're marching along the curve.

18:30.640 --> 18:34.320
Very concretely consider, for example,

18:34.320 --> 18:38.800
the problem of planning the trajectory

18:38.800 --> 18:40.680
for the joint of a manipulator.

18:40.680 --> 18:43.000
Let's call this joint vector theta.

18:44.200 --> 18:48.960
So for example, a typical way of parameterizing

18:48.960 --> 18:53.080
straight line motion is to say that your joints

18:53.080 --> 18:57.240
have to go from an initial condition T start

18:57.240 --> 19:00.000
plus S, which is curve length,

19:01.320 --> 19:05.440
times T end minus theta start.

19:10.480 --> 19:12.680
This will be like a straight line trajectory,

19:12.680 --> 19:15.120
basically, erratically in a path.

19:15.120 --> 19:19.360
S is a variable that belongs to zero and one.

19:19.360 --> 19:23.480
If S is equal to zero, then you are at the start position.

19:23.480 --> 19:26.680
If S is equal to one, theta start and theta start

19:26.680 --> 19:28.920
cancel out, then you are at the end position.

19:31.120 --> 19:35.120
So in this case, there is not even computation of the path.

19:35.120 --> 19:38.000
You just say, I want to do a straight path.

19:38.000 --> 19:42.440
Then, you say, okay, so that's a path I want to follow.

19:42.440 --> 19:45.120
What is the time schedule that I use

19:45.120 --> 19:50.120
in order to march along this path?

19:50.360 --> 19:53.600
Well, one of the easiest time schedule

19:53.600 --> 19:58.600
is a linear time schedule, C T.

20:01.880 --> 20:04.160
Actually, C T over T.

20:04.160 --> 20:08.160
So that's, yeah, T over T,

20:08.160 --> 20:11.880
such that S of zero is equal to zero

20:11.880 --> 20:14.000
and S of one is equal to one.

20:16.040 --> 20:18.440
Sorry, S of T, capital T, is equal to one.

20:19.440 --> 20:20.920
So what's the idea?

20:20.920 --> 20:24.800
That now, if you take the derivative of the theta

20:24.800 --> 20:28.400
with respect to time, what is the derivative equal to?

20:30.040 --> 20:34.760
It's the derivative of theta S with respect to S

20:34.760 --> 20:39.760
times S dot, meaning derivative S with respect to T.

20:39.760 --> 20:44.760
So this is equal to one over T,

20:55.200 --> 20:58.600
which basically means that capital T is a tuning parameter.

20:58.600 --> 21:02.240
So it's how much time you allocate

21:02.240 --> 21:04.440
in order to traverse this curve.

21:04.440 --> 21:07.760
As you increase T,

21:08.760 --> 21:11.240
you decrease theta dot.

21:11.240 --> 21:14.400
And so the idea is that maybe you have upper limits

21:14.400 --> 21:16.840
on how quickly your joint can move.

21:16.840 --> 21:18.200
Usually there are acceleration limits,

21:18.200 --> 21:22.120
but anyway, upper limit on how quickly your joints can move.

21:22.120 --> 21:23.560
Then you can fulfill them

21:23.560 --> 21:26.040
by appropriately tuning capital T.

21:26.040 --> 21:28.760
You make a capital T large enough

21:28.760 --> 21:31.200
so that the maximum value of theta dot

21:31.200 --> 21:34.960
along the trajectory is below the upper bound.

21:34.960 --> 21:37.400
Say, 10 hundred per second, I don't know.

21:38.760 --> 21:39.920
Does it make sense?

21:42.160 --> 21:43.880
As I said, the idea is simple,

21:43.880 --> 21:45.440
but you want to spend a little bit of time

21:45.440 --> 21:47.920
to really get familiar to it, with it.

21:49.320 --> 21:51.400
So in full generality,

21:53.000 --> 21:54.720
the idea is

21:57.360 --> 21:59.560
that we view a trajectory

22:01.200 --> 22:04.160
where there is a X, but just to be consistent

22:04.160 --> 22:05.560
with the part that we are considering

22:05.720 --> 22:08.160
differential flatness, let's call it Z.

22:08.160 --> 22:11.160
Z of T, we view it as a function of S of T.

22:12.840 --> 22:14.800
So that the zeta dot T

22:15.960 --> 22:17.640
is equal to the derivative of X

22:17.640 --> 22:19.960
with respect to S times S dot.

22:20.920 --> 22:25.280
By appropriately choosing S of T, the time schedule,

22:25.280 --> 22:29.760
I can accordingly change the derivative of Z.

22:29.760 --> 22:32.680
And then I would choose the schedule

22:32.680 --> 22:35.240
in such a way that properties on the derivative

22:35.800 --> 22:37.680
in terms of upper bounds are satisfied.

22:38.680 --> 22:41.680
Basically, it's low the time just enough

22:41.680 --> 22:44.760
so that all the bounds are satisfied.

22:44.760 --> 22:47.920
In particular, the bounds that usually we're interested in

22:47.920 --> 22:52.920
are input bounds, so the bounds on your control inputs.

22:53.400 --> 22:57.560
For example, going back to the case of the unicycle,

23:00.560 --> 23:04.040
we said that the input bounds are that omega T

23:04.040 --> 23:06.920
has to be less than omega max,

23:06.920 --> 23:11.640
and V T has to be less than or equal to V max.

23:13.360 --> 23:14.840
Well, in magnitude.

23:17.720 --> 23:22.720
So V T is equal to the derivative

23:23.800 --> 23:27.840
of X T over D T,

23:29.080 --> 23:33.880
which is equal if we view V as a function of S of T

23:34.720 --> 23:39.720
as derivative of X of S over D S times S dot

23:43.760 --> 23:47.440
equal to V of S times S dot.

23:49.240 --> 23:54.240
So if S represents arc length,

23:54.240 --> 23:59.240
again, the arc length is at the natural parameterization

24:04.560 --> 24:05.640
for the car.

24:05.640 --> 24:09.920
It tells you for each point what is the distance

24:09.920 --> 24:13.360
of that point from the origin of the car along the path.

24:14.960 --> 24:18.440
So if that is the case, then interpretation is as follows.

24:18.560 --> 24:23.560
This is your car, X of S.

24:30.000 --> 24:34.360
V of S is the velocity that your vehicle is

24:34.360 --> 24:39.360
when it is at a distance S from the origin, S start.

24:43.920 --> 24:47.760
So you compute the path with associated V of S,

24:47.760 --> 24:51.060
and then for the true input, which is V of T,

24:51.060 --> 24:55.940
you modulate it by choosing a scaling function S.

24:58.960 --> 25:02.360
As I said, typical choices for S are a linear scaling

25:02.360 --> 25:05.440
function where the tuning is with respect to the horizon,

25:05.440 --> 25:07.920
or maybe a polynomial scaling function.

25:07.920 --> 25:10.600
For example, in the case of manipulators,

25:10.600 --> 25:14.400
oftentimes one uses cubic polynomial

25:14.400 --> 25:16.120
to represent scaling function.

25:16.120 --> 25:17.560
This is for speed.

25:17.560 --> 25:20.520
Let's look at angular speed.

25:25.800 --> 25:30.800
So omega T is equal to the derivative of theta T over T,

25:39.320 --> 25:43.600
which is equal to derivative of theta with respect to S

25:43.600 --> 25:44.960
times S dot.

25:45.800 --> 25:50.360
From here, we see that S dot is equal to,

25:52.560 --> 25:53.940
just to be consistent with notation,

25:53.940 --> 25:57.760
this is a, do I use capital B or small b?

25:57.760 --> 25:58.600
Yeah, small b.

25:59.440 --> 26:03.200
So this small b, and this small b.

26:08.740 --> 26:13.220
So S dot is equal to V T over V S.

26:15.160 --> 26:20.160
So this is equal to D T in the S times V T over V S,

26:24.380 --> 26:29.380
equal to theta of S V T over V S.

26:35.400 --> 26:39.280
So for example, so what we will do is then

26:39.280 --> 26:42.160
to choose the time schedule S in such a way

26:42.160 --> 26:46.160
that both your velocity constraint

26:46.160 --> 26:48.520
and angular constraints are satisfied.

26:48.520 --> 26:51.200
Basically, you slow down the motion

26:51.200 --> 26:55.660
until the maximum V and maximum omega along the path

26:55.660 --> 26:57.400
is below that input.

26:57.400 --> 26:59.960
In the case of the P set, you do actually

27:03.020 --> 27:05.600
something a bit different.

27:05.600 --> 27:07.880
What you do, that is like a twist that I made

27:07.880 --> 27:09.760
for the P set to make it more engaging.

27:09.760 --> 27:11.040
Both are useful.

27:11.040 --> 27:14.840
So what you do in the P set is not to set

27:14.840 --> 27:18.240
the time scaling law S of T directly.

27:18.240 --> 27:23.240
Instead, I ask you to readjust the velocity

27:26.080 --> 27:28.360
in the arc length parameterization

27:29.560 --> 27:32.680
in such a way that the input bounds are satisfied.

27:32.680 --> 27:36.760
In particular, you want to make sure that V of S

27:36.800 --> 27:41.320
is less than P max and omega S is less than omega max.

27:41.320 --> 27:43.640
Keep in mind that you have to use this formula

27:43.640 --> 27:44.840
to ensure the constraint.

27:46.600 --> 27:51.600
So once you compute a new velocity profile along the path,

27:53.720 --> 27:54.840
we're not changing the path.

27:54.840 --> 27:59.840
We're just computing a new velocity profile along the path.

28:00.520 --> 28:03.700
Implicitly, you are defining a new time schedule.

28:04.660 --> 28:08.440
When you get the time at which you get to some point

28:08.440 --> 28:11.900
along the path is, of course, a function of the speed,

28:11.900 --> 28:13.700
the speed that you're following along the path.

28:13.700 --> 28:17.900
And in particular, just related to the integral of velocity

28:17.900 --> 28:19.000
with respect to space.

28:20.120 --> 28:21.120
Does that make sense?

28:23.580 --> 28:24.820
So it's just a different twist.

28:24.820 --> 28:29.820
You can adjust your speed, your input bounds

28:30.700 --> 28:34.540
by either adjusting the schedule directly

28:34.540 --> 28:39.540
or adjusting your input directly,

28:39.580 --> 28:42.500
which implicitly define a new time schedule.

28:42.500 --> 28:45.180
And that's what you do in the first problem of PSEC-1.

28:46.340 --> 28:49.260
It seems like the second one is more natural.

28:49.260 --> 28:53.540
Is once you understand what you're doing is more natural,

28:53.540 --> 28:56.020
but in terms of first being exposed

28:56.020 --> 28:57.620
to the notion of time scaling,

28:57.620 --> 28:59.020
I will claim it is more natural.

28:59.060 --> 29:01.900
That's why I decided to explain it in this way

29:01.900 --> 29:03.540
because I think it's more pedagogical.

29:03.540 --> 29:04.660
But I agree with you.

29:04.660 --> 29:07.620
Once you get the hang of it, then it's more natural to.

29:08.620 --> 29:10.580
Do you want to understand the dynamic reduces

29:10.580 --> 29:13.820
in the real world and then reverse engineer the actual path?

29:13.820 --> 29:15.580
Yeah, it's a much more direct way.

29:15.580 --> 29:17.100
Okay, gotcha.

29:17.100 --> 29:20.140
And that's why I'm telling you to do it that way.

29:20.140 --> 29:21.740
Any questions here?

29:21.740 --> 29:22.580
Yeah.

29:22.580 --> 29:24.420
Just real quick, in the equation up there,

29:24.420 --> 29:28.780
it says, so you have M as the time derivative of our path

29:29.500 --> 29:32.620
Hold on, you have the time derivative, so here, you mean?

29:32.620 --> 29:33.460
Yes.

29:33.460 --> 29:35.300
And in the next step, we just say S of T,

29:35.300 --> 29:37.380
but should it be T of S?

29:37.380 --> 29:38.220
No.

29:38.220 --> 29:41.900
Because we're integrating over the path and then saying like.

29:41.900 --> 29:45.500
No, so here is actually different

29:45.500 --> 29:47.260
because here you are differentiating

29:47.260 --> 29:49.580
with respect to the arc length.

29:49.580 --> 29:51.620
Let's say that S is the arc length.

29:51.620 --> 29:56.140
So you want to differentiate with respect to the value of X

29:57.140 --> 30:00.420
with respect to the value of X at the position

30:00.420 --> 30:03.260
along the curve that correspond to a given time T.

30:03.260 --> 30:05.660
Okay, but then the time is not the same time

30:05.660 --> 30:07.700
that we have in the one before?

30:08.740 --> 30:10.420
No, the time T is the same.

30:10.420 --> 30:11.260
Okay.

30:11.260 --> 30:12.100
Yeah, no, of course.

30:12.100 --> 30:15.300
So basically, the idea is to view X.

30:16.580 --> 30:18.420
So basically, the question of Paul is,

30:18.420 --> 30:23.420
should we say, should we view X of T as X of S T

30:26.260 --> 30:29.140
or X of T S?

30:32.780 --> 30:33.900
That's the thing, right?

30:33.900 --> 30:34.740
Yeah.

30:34.740 --> 30:37.260
No, that's an interpretation that we give.

30:37.260 --> 30:42.260
So we view X as indirectly depending on T

30:42.780 --> 30:46.580
through the arc length transformation.

30:46.580 --> 30:51.580
So we say, given time T, where are we along the path?

30:52.740 --> 30:54.460
And based on where we're along the path,

30:54.460 --> 30:56.300
what is the corresponding value of X?

30:59.340 --> 31:03.420
So that's how I set up the time scaling transformation.

31:04.300 --> 31:06.420
If we view it this other way,

31:06.420 --> 31:10.580
then it will not make sense to compute the derivative

31:10.580 --> 31:13.060
with respect to the path S,

31:13.060 --> 31:15.580
because now this is the real independent variable,

31:15.580 --> 31:17.540
so the composition of derivatives

31:17.540 --> 31:18.980
will be the other way around.

31:20.740 --> 31:21.580
But just intuition.

31:21.580 --> 31:25.100
So the idea is, for a given time T,

31:25.100 --> 31:27.980
there is a corresponding value of S,

31:27.980 --> 31:29.860
where I am at a time T.

31:31.220 --> 31:32.940
And then, once I'm here,

31:32.940 --> 31:35.100
I have a corresponding value of X of S.

31:35.100 --> 31:40.100
So basically, I separate X of T in X of S and S of T.

31:42.380 --> 31:44.140
As I said, it's simple,

31:44.140 --> 31:46.580
but you have to spend a little bit of time on it.

31:46.580 --> 31:48.580
It's deceptively simple.

31:48.660 --> 31:50.820
What is the opposite of T?

31:52.940 --> 31:54.380
Here?

31:54.380 --> 31:58.780
Yeah, so this is a possible time in log.

31:58.780 --> 32:01.620
So T is the time, the variable.

32:01.620 --> 32:04.660
Capital T is the horizon, as I discussed it before,

32:04.660 --> 32:06.700
so how much time we allocate

32:06.700 --> 32:08.580
in order to traverse this path,

32:08.580 --> 32:11.020
which is what ultimately we're going to tune

32:11.020 --> 32:13.620
in order to satisfy the input bounds,

32:13.620 --> 32:16.260
for example, V and omega.

32:16.260 --> 32:20.060
And L is the length of the path,

32:20.060 --> 32:22.340
which means that when T is,

32:22.340 --> 32:24.380
T is not fixed, it's something that you tune.

32:24.380 --> 32:27.740
When T is equal to the time horizon, capital T,

32:27.740 --> 32:30.100
then S of capital T is equal to L,

32:30.100 --> 32:32.140
which means that you are at the end of the path.

32:35.820 --> 32:37.020
Yeah?

32:37.020 --> 32:39.940
You said earlier that a common choice for S is arc length.

32:39.940 --> 32:43.420
Are there other common ways to parametrize?

32:43.420 --> 32:44.620
Is it de facto standard?

32:44.620 --> 32:46.700
Because it's the one that really makes sense,

32:46.700 --> 32:48.140
as we were discussing before.

32:49.260 --> 32:52.820
You want to decouple the geometric description,

32:52.820 --> 32:56.060
which is really captured by the notion of arc length,

32:56.060 --> 32:57.180
from the time schedule,

32:57.180 --> 33:00.020
so that at each point of the path,

33:00.020 --> 33:02.660
you select the velocity you want to be,

33:03.820 --> 33:08.820
and then you implicitly compute a time schedule

33:09.180 --> 33:10.900
that ensures that that is the case.

33:15.620 --> 33:16.460
All right.

33:18.860 --> 33:21.140
So what have we done so far?

33:21.140 --> 33:23.740
So what we've done so far is,

33:23.740 --> 33:25.420
starting from the two-step design

33:25.420 --> 33:26.740
that we discussed last time,

33:26.740 --> 33:28.700
we said we first are going to compute

33:28.700 --> 33:29.940
an open-loop trajectory.

33:29.940 --> 33:33.420
Open-loop trajectory means open-loop sequence of controls,

33:33.420 --> 33:36.180
and corresponding state trajectory X.

33:36.180 --> 33:37.780
The correspondence is through

33:37.780 --> 33:39.580
the differential equation of motion.

33:42.100 --> 33:43.840
This is what we've done so far.

33:43.880 --> 33:45.640
But as we discussed last time,

33:45.640 --> 33:48.240
this strategy tends to be fragile,

33:48.240 --> 33:52.240
because it does not have any mechanism

33:52.240 --> 33:54.760
to constantly check how well you're doing,

33:54.760 --> 33:56.920
and correct for possible deviations

33:56.920 --> 33:59.680
due to modern mismatches, noise, and so on.

33:59.680 --> 34:01.920
So we want to augment the strategy

34:01.920 --> 34:06.920
with a tracking controller that gives us an extra level,

34:07.480 --> 34:08.880
that give us a level of robustness

34:08.880 --> 34:10.560
toward modern mismatches.

34:11.480 --> 34:13.000
Now, the tracking controller

34:13.000 --> 34:14.560
is indeed a closed-loop controller,

34:14.560 --> 34:16.940
which means that the control depends

34:16.940 --> 34:18.960
on the current value of the state.

34:18.960 --> 34:22.440
But usually, it's designed to work locally,

34:22.440 --> 34:25.920
so basically in a tube around the nominal trajectory.

34:25.920 --> 34:29.060
Here, D, the subscript of D, means desired.

34:29.060 --> 34:31.660
Basically, desired trajectory, nominal trajectory.

34:31.660 --> 34:34.560
We discussed how to compute the open-loop trajectory

34:34.560 --> 34:36.660
through differential correctness techniques.

34:36.660 --> 34:40.720
We had discussed how to account for input bound constraints

34:40.720 --> 34:42.640
by using the notion of time scaling.

34:43.240 --> 34:47.380
Now we are in a position to move to this piece over here.

34:48.320 --> 34:51.440
This piece over here is problem one in your P set.

34:51.440 --> 34:54.780
This piece over here is problem three in your P set.

34:56.280 --> 34:58.720
As I said, trajectory tracking entails computing

34:58.720 --> 35:00.900
a closed-loop controller,

35:00.900 --> 35:04.080
something that maps the current value of the state,

35:04.080 --> 35:08.520
and usually the difference between where you are

35:08.520 --> 35:11.460
and where you would like to be,

35:11.460 --> 35:13.160
into a corrective control action.

35:14.280 --> 35:16.400
Now, there are many ways of designing

35:16.400 --> 35:19.860
a trajectory tracking controller.

35:19.860 --> 35:22.480
The simplest one, that actually works pretty well,

35:22.480 --> 35:23.880
is a purely

35:25.680 --> 35:26.840
geometric approach.

35:29.480 --> 35:34.040
Whereby, if this is your desired trajectory, X of D,

35:35.040 --> 35:36.440
and you're here,

35:37.400 --> 35:42.060
you may consider the corrective action

35:42.060 --> 35:46.260
that tells you how to intercept.

35:46.260 --> 35:51.080
So, you would like to be here at time T,

35:51.080 --> 35:53.160
and you're here at time T.

35:53.160 --> 35:56.720
So simple tracking control law is the so-called

35:56.720 --> 35:58.120
pursue control law,

35:58.120 --> 36:00.800
where you have an additional control term

36:00.800 --> 36:03.700
whose goal is try to bring you back

36:03.700 --> 36:06.200
along, say, a geometric path, like a straight line.

36:07.560 --> 36:12.560
Say, T is plus delta T seconds ahead of you in time.

36:16.000 --> 36:20.160
So basically, it's like a donkey that is chasing the carrot.

36:20.160 --> 36:22.640
That is an example of, if you are tracking.

36:22.640 --> 36:24.720
So here, this is the carrot.

36:27.080 --> 36:29.240
Well, I don't know how to design a carrot.

