with the fact that it's tangential at that point.

36:50.760 --> 36:51.600
No, no, no.

36:51.600 --> 36:53.200
And of course, it's something that moves.

36:53.200 --> 36:54.620
As you move, it moves.

36:57.360 --> 36:58.920
Of course, there is a mathematical formulation

36:58.920 --> 36:59.760
of all of this.

37:00.200 --> 37:04.120
The key point is that it's a fairly simple idea.

37:04.120 --> 37:06.800
And as all simple ideas, they tend to work pretty well.

37:07.840 --> 37:10.880
The second approach is linearization.

37:10.880 --> 37:15.440
So the idea of linearization is that you want

37:15.440 --> 37:18.480
to turn the problem into a linear one

37:18.480 --> 37:21.040
to which you can apply linear control techniques.

37:21.040 --> 37:23.400
For those of you that are not familiar with controls,

37:23.400 --> 37:26.000
linear controls is way easier than the linear control.

37:26.000 --> 37:30.000
So that's what people want to apply techniques

37:30.000 --> 37:30.840
from linear controls.

37:30.840 --> 37:34.480
Linear controls means control theory applied to systems

37:34.480 --> 37:37.360
whose differential equation is linear.

37:38.680 --> 37:41.160
There are two ways of linearizing the system.

37:41.160 --> 37:44.280
One is exact linearization,

37:44.280 --> 37:46.800
which is very related to the notion of differential,

37:46.800 --> 37:48.960
very much related to the notion of differential flatness.

37:48.960 --> 37:50.740
We're going to discuss it in a second.

37:50.740 --> 37:54.600
The second one is inexact linearization.

37:54.600 --> 37:57.880
So the idea is that since for the purposes

37:57.880 --> 38:02.880
of trajectory tracking, if nothing terrible happens,

38:03.280 --> 38:07.880
you're typically very close to your nominal trajectory.

38:07.880 --> 38:12.880
So you may think of linearizing the equations of motion

38:14.680 --> 38:17.820
around your nominal trajectory.

38:17.820 --> 38:19.700
The linearized system is now linear,

38:19.700 --> 38:21.680
and then you can apply linear control techniques

38:21.680 --> 38:24.800
for designing your trajectory tracking controller.

38:24.800 --> 38:26.760
Linearization basically means that

38:27.900 --> 38:30.040
if you have a non-linear function,

38:30.040 --> 38:31.440
and you know that you are at some point

38:31.440 --> 38:34.360
along that linear function, if you move locally,

38:34.360 --> 38:37.680
so if you stay close to that point,

38:37.680 --> 38:39.940
you can approximate the non-linear function

38:39.940 --> 38:41.600
with just a line.

38:41.600 --> 38:45.760
And then do the control, design the control strategy

38:45.760 --> 38:47.840
with respect to the simplified model

38:47.840 --> 38:49.640
where basically everything becomes a line.

38:49.640 --> 38:53.040
Anyway, if you're familiar with controls,

38:53.040 --> 38:54.040
this is an obvious concept.

38:54.040 --> 38:55.720
If you're not, it's a bit obscure.

38:55.720 --> 38:58.520
Just to let you know that this is also a possibility.

38:59.880 --> 39:02.520
The third possibility relies

39:02.520 --> 39:04.560
on non-linear control techniques.

39:04.560 --> 39:09.560
So you tackle the problem without simplification.

39:11.020 --> 39:12.940
Usually this is very hard to do,

39:12.940 --> 39:16.400
and seldom people do it.

39:16.400 --> 39:17.660
If you can do it, it's great,

39:17.660 --> 39:19.720
because you're not doing any approximation,

39:19.720 --> 39:21.460
you're not doing any weird transformation,

39:21.460 --> 39:23.740
but most of the time you can't.

39:23.740 --> 39:27.640
The fourth one is by using optimization-based techniques.

39:27.640 --> 39:31.140
So the tracking controller becomes the solution

39:31.140 --> 39:33.320
to an optimization algorithm.

39:33.320 --> 39:36.820
This is really the realm of model predictive control.

39:36.820 --> 39:37.980
It has a number of advantages.

39:37.980 --> 39:41.220
It can handle large variety of constraints and so on.

39:42.900 --> 39:44.800
But this is beyond the scope of this class.

39:44.800 --> 39:46.580
So what we're going to discuss in this class

39:46.660 --> 39:49.240
is the idea of exact linearization

39:49.240 --> 39:51.880
that is very much related to differential flatness.

39:51.880 --> 39:56.180
We're not going to discuss the other solution strategies.

39:56.180 --> 39:58.620
Just wanted to, you know,

39:58.620 --> 40:00.100
by now you know the philosophy of this class.

40:00.100 --> 40:01.660
I want to give you the high-level overview

40:01.660 --> 40:03.940
of all the tools that are out there,

40:03.940 --> 40:08.140
and then we go deep into one tool that is pedagogical

40:08.140 --> 40:10.740
and is of interesting practice, and is widely used.

40:12.060 --> 40:15.340
So in particular, this is what we're going to discuss now.

40:15.340 --> 40:18.380
Any questions before we do our deep dive

40:18.380 --> 40:19.660
in exact linearization?

40:24.140 --> 40:25.180
All right.

40:25.180 --> 40:28.860
So the idea of exact linearization

40:28.860 --> 40:31.500
is that for some systems,

40:31.500 --> 40:33.380
and in particular the system for which this work

40:33.380 --> 40:36.140
is exactly the class of differential flat systems,

40:36.140 --> 40:39.860
it is possible through a change of coordinates

40:39.860 --> 40:42.660
and through a transformational control input

40:42.660 --> 40:46.340
to transform the system into a linear system,

40:46.340 --> 40:49.060
meaning one that looks like this

40:50.660 --> 40:51.900
in a way that is one-to-one,

40:51.900 --> 40:53.580
meaning the solution to the linear system

40:53.580 --> 40:56.860
can be mapped into the solution of the non-linear system

40:56.860 --> 40:59.060
and vice versa, which means that then you can do

40:59.060 --> 41:01.300
control design for the linear system

41:01.300 --> 41:05.520
and then map the controller that you have

41:05.520 --> 41:09.060
into the non-linear controller for the non-linear system.

41:09.060 --> 41:11.240
Very simple example.

41:12.660 --> 41:17.660
This is super simple too.

41:19.300 --> 41:21.580
So let's assume that you have a system x dot

41:21.580 --> 41:24.180
equal fx plus u.

41:24.180 --> 41:27.020
Well, I don't know why v came out, but say u.

41:28.540 --> 41:30.060
And I tell you to control the system.

41:30.060 --> 41:34.100
So find a control history u that drives the state

41:34.100 --> 41:36.500
of the system to the origin, for example.

41:37.620 --> 41:40.840
Now, f is in general a non-linear function of x.

41:40.840 --> 41:43.360
So you might start thinking, this is complicated

41:43.360 --> 41:45.360
because in general, I don't know the solution

41:45.360 --> 41:47.120
to this differential equation.

41:47.120 --> 41:49.960
How do I really compute u and so on?

41:49.960 --> 41:52.720
But then you might just say, well,

41:52.720 --> 41:57.720
let's set f of x plus u equal v, a virtual control input.

42:00.860 --> 42:05.860
Now, you design v so that x goes to zero.

42:06.960 --> 42:08.740
This is the simplest possible differential equation

42:08.740 --> 42:09.680
that you can have.

42:11.680 --> 42:14.440
And then in the real controller u,

42:14.440 --> 42:17.100
that you will need to use for the true system

42:17.100 --> 42:22.100
to go to the origin, will be v minus f of x.

42:25.760 --> 42:26.960
So what have you done here?

42:26.960 --> 42:31.280
Basically, you are computing an input u

42:31.280 --> 42:33.200
that cancels the non-linearity

42:34.900 --> 42:37.220
and replace the system with a linear structure.

42:38.220 --> 42:40.500
So that now you can just do the control

42:40.500 --> 42:42.540
for respect to v.

42:42.540 --> 42:43.980
So in particular, you can choose v

42:43.980 --> 42:45.780
that makes sure that you converge to zero

42:45.780 --> 42:48.220
with some desired convergence rate and so on.

42:48.220 --> 42:50.260
And then with just the inverse transformation,

42:50.260 --> 42:53.100
you get the input for the true system.

42:53.100 --> 42:54.060
Does it make sense?

42:55.500 --> 42:57.220
This is called exact linearization

42:57.220 --> 42:59.340
because you're not taking any approximation.

42:59.340 --> 43:02.500
You're not doing like in exact linearization,

43:03.420 --> 43:06.360
a Taylor series expansion of your differential equation

43:06.360 --> 43:10.460
and then you truncate the Taylor system up to some order.

43:10.460 --> 43:11.640
You're not doing that.

43:11.640 --> 43:12.480
This is exact.

43:13.680 --> 43:16.520
The problem is that district does not work

43:16.520 --> 43:18.560
for all possible systems.

43:18.560 --> 43:22.360
It works exactly for the differential flat systems.

43:22.360 --> 43:23.980
In particular, one can show

43:23.980 --> 43:26.300
that every differential flat systems

43:26.300 --> 43:28.720
can be equivalently transformed

43:28.720 --> 43:33.720
by a non-linear transformation into a linear system

43:33.760 --> 43:36.760
where by z is the flat output as usual

43:36.760 --> 43:39.800
and it's q plus one derivative is equal to w,

43:39.800 --> 43:42.120
which is the mutual control input.

43:42.120 --> 43:44.100
The fact that there is a tight connection

43:44.100 --> 43:48.400
between feedback linearization and differential flatness

43:48.400 --> 43:50.440
has not been proven here rigorously

43:50.440 --> 43:52.560
but shouldn't come as a total surprise.

43:52.560 --> 43:54.440
As we already discussed, differential flatness

43:54.440 --> 43:57.720
basically removes all the non-linearity from the problem.

43:57.720 --> 43:59.080
So it's not at all surprising

43:59.080 --> 44:01.000
that there is an additional theoretical result

44:01.000 --> 44:03.400
that says that actually you can also give to it

44:03.960 --> 44:05.920
a dynamic interpretation.

44:05.920 --> 44:08.860
But if you want to dig deeper,

44:09.800 --> 44:12.700
I suggest to look at this book, Levina 2009.

44:15.920 --> 44:17.720
Key point here is that the differential flat system

44:17.720 --> 44:20.680
can be translated into a linear system

44:20.680 --> 44:22.120
in an equivalent fashion.

44:22.120 --> 44:23.780
The mappings are really the

44:23.780 --> 44:25.720
differential flat output mappings.

44:25.720 --> 44:27.520
I'm going to give an example in the next slide

44:27.520 --> 44:29.160
so that this becomes much clearer.

44:30.060 --> 44:30.900
Yeah.

44:31.920 --> 44:32.760
Okay.

44:33.480 --> 44:35.000
So if that is the case,

44:35.000 --> 44:38.520
so we have been able to translate to the non-linear system

44:38.520 --> 44:40.460
in an equivalent linear system

44:40.460 --> 44:42.880
through a non-linear transformation.

44:42.880 --> 44:44.880
We'll view it as a change of coordinates,

44:44.880 --> 44:47.700
but it's non-linear in order to remove the non-linearities.

44:47.700 --> 44:49.560
Once we have the linear system,

44:49.560 --> 44:51.760
then we can apply classical linear control techniques

44:51.760 --> 44:53.080
for tracking.

44:53.080 --> 44:56.400
In particular, the simplest possible way to do it

44:56.400 --> 45:00.160
and actually the way that people do it is

45:00.160 --> 45:05.160
you have the flat output, Z,

45:05.820 --> 45:09.500
you have the desired flat output ZDT,

45:09.500 --> 45:11.520
and then you have the actual flat output,

45:11.520 --> 45:13.240
so the one that is actually realized

45:13.240 --> 45:16.080
due to disturbances, model mismatches, and so on.

45:16.080 --> 45:17.220
Z of T.

45:18.880 --> 45:22.360
Then we define an error signal, EI,

45:24.960 --> 45:29.960
as ZIT minus ZDT.

45:30.560 --> 45:33.400
ZID or IDT.

45:36.440 --> 45:40.640
I is the i-th component of the flat output, Z,

45:40.640 --> 45:41.880
which is a general vector.

45:43.580 --> 45:44.420
Okay?

45:45.680 --> 45:48.360
So when you take the derivative,

45:48.360 --> 45:50.880
actually the derivative up to q plus one,

45:50.880 --> 45:54.720
so Eq plus one,

45:55.720 --> 45:58.040
EI T,

45:58.040 --> 46:03.040
is equal to ZI q plus one T

46:03.120 --> 46:07.920
minus ZID q plus one T.

46:09.840 --> 46:11.880
So this is equal to,

46:11.880 --> 46:14.720
we just said that the dynamics

46:14.720 --> 46:16.060
in the flat output space are linear,

46:16.060 --> 46:17.920
so this is equal to the control input,

46:17.920 --> 46:21.560
the i-th component of the control input, Wi,

46:21.560 --> 46:23.760
and is equal to Wi desired.

46:24.080 --> 46:29.080
So how do you choose Wi in such a way

46:29.560 --> 46:32.040
that the error dynamics go to zero?

46:32.040 --> 46:34.040
Error dynamics goes to zero implies

46:34.040 --> 46:35.840
that the tracking error goes to zero,

46:35.840 --> 46:40.680
because it implies that ZI goes to ZID for each i.

46:40.680 --> 46:43.720
Basically implies that the actual flat output

46:43.720 --> 46:46.060
goes to the desired output asymptotically.

46:47.280 --> 46:48.120
So what do we do?

46:54.400 --> 46:59.400
You just set Vi equal to Vid so that you cancel this term,

47:02.320 --> 47:05.260
and then you add a stable dynamics

47:05.260 --> 47:07.300
for the error system to the system,

47:07.300 --> 47:12.300
meaning minus the sum from j equal zero,

47:14.600 --> 47:19.600
q, Kij Ej T.

47:19.600 --> 47:24.600
So that the resulting differential equation,

47:32.560 --> 47:34.440
so that the resulting differential equation

47:34.440 --> 47:39.440
is Ei q plus one T is equal,

47:44.420 --> 47:46.760
so the resulting equation is Ei q plus one T

47:46.760 --> 47:51.760
plus sum of j equals zero to q, Kij Ej T equals zero.

47:56.920 --> 47:58.680
So basically with this choice,

47:58.680 --> 48:03.360
you have been able to derive an equation

48:03.360 --> 48:06.160
for your error dynamics, which is linear,

48:06.160 --> 48:09.520
and what you have to determine are these gains K.

48:09.520 --> 48:12.200
How would you pick those gains K?

48:12.200 --> 48:13.040
Kij?

48:16.760 --> 48:21.760
Sorry, Ei, jth derivative, Ei, jth derivative.

48:30.660 --> 48:32.160
How do you compute those gains?

48:33.640 --> 48:36.880
Basically, how do you design the tacking control law?

48:36.880 --> 48:38.400
You want the error to go to zero.

48:38.400 --> 48:40.680
So what do you want for this differential equation?

48:40.680 --> 48:42.320
What structural property you want?

48:43.520 --> 48:45.800
So if you know about linear differential equations,

48:45.800 --> 48:48.180
or you should know about linear differential equations,

48:48.180 --> 48:52.320
you want to compute the gains K in such a way

48:52.320 --> 48:54.820
that the eigenvalues of differential equations

48:54.820 --> 48:56.560
have negative real parts,

48:56.560 --> 48:58.240
which means that the error dynamics

48:58.240 --> 49:00.040
and its derivatives converge to zero.

49:01.640 --> 49:04.600
If you're not familiar with the linear differential equation,

49:04.600 --> 49:06.120
it's fine.

49:06.120 --> 49:09.240
The key message here is that there are very well

49:09.240 --> 49:12.720
understood techniques to compute the gains K,

49:12.720 --> 49:16.120
such that this differential equation is stable,

49:16.120 --> 49:18.240
which means that E, the variable,

49:18.240 --> 49:20.400
and all its derivatives converge to zero.

49:25.960 --> 49:28.680
So what is the resulting tacking control law?

49:30.920 --> 49:35.280
Is Wi equal to Wi desired,

49:35.280 --> 49:37.840
so the desired control input, which we know,

49:38.840 --> 49:42.300
minus the superposition of E and its derivative.

49:43.560 --> 49:45.740
Which we can compute because we assume

49:45.740 --> 49:48.280
that we have access to the flat outputs

49:48.280 --> 49:49.120
and its derivatives.

49:49.120 --> 49:50.640
It's something that we can measure.

49:52.400 --> 49:53.760
I'm going to give an example in a second

49:53.760 --> 49:55.320
so that it becomes super clear.

49:57.240 --> 50:01.420
Well, yes, so Q,

50:03.440 --> 50:08.440
so if you remember from the definition of the flat outputs,

50:08.440 --> 50:13.360
the flat outputs are a transformation

50:13.360 --> 50:16.360
from the state U and its derivative up to Q.

50:16.360 --> 50:17.720
So that's the Q.

50:17.720 --> 50:21.720
Now a good question is, if you give me a linear system,

50:21.720 --> 50:22.880
is it differentially flat?

50:22.880 --> 50:25.580
As we said, this is a very difficult question to answer.

50:25.580 --> 50:27.420
The reason why it's a very difficult question to answer

50:27.420 --> 50:30.840
is that a priori, nobody tells you

50:32.120 --> 50:34.360
