56:59.920 --> 57:01.520
if these two are positive,

57:01.520 --> 57:04.160
then all the roots are negative and I'm done.

57:04.160 --> 57:06.720
As I said, if you're not familiar with differential,

57:06.720 --> 57:08.200
linear differential equations, it's fine.

57:08.200 --> 57:10.840
It's suffice to say that for the second order

57:10.840 --> 57:12.120
linear differential equation,

57:12.120 --> 57:15.620
just pick the gains that are positive and you're fine.

57:17.560 --> 57:21.220
The specific values affect how quickly you go to zero.

57:22.220 --> 57:24.700
So you might think, well, why don't you pick like k

57:24.700 --> 57:27.400
in such a way that you go very quickly to zero,

57:27.400 --> 57:30.100
is because oftentimes that translates into,

57:30.100 --> 57:32.820
that violates saturation bounds for your inputs.

57:32.820 --> 57:34.740
So if you do so, then you have to do time scaling

57:34.740 --> 57:36.740
in order to scale everything back

57:36.740 --> 57:38.740
so that you fulfill the bounds.

57:40.440 --> 57:41.300
But so that's neat, right?

57:41.300 --> 57:44.700
Because in the same framework of differential flatness,

57:44.700 --> 57:48.580
we know how to do open loop trajectory planning

57:48.620 --> 57:51.260
and how to design a tracking controller

57:52.140 --> 57:53.640
in the same framework.

57:53.640 --> 57:55.700
So it's pretty remarkable.

57:55.700 --> 57:56.760
Any questions here?

57:58.140 --> 58:00.220
Again, this is not super complicated,

58:00.220 --> 58:03.680
but I encourage you to spend a little bit of time

58:03.680 --> 58:05.420
to familiarize with these concepts.

58:07.220 --> 58:10.180
Many ideas with differential flatness to translate

58:10.180 --> 58:12.180
the trajectory planning problem

58:12.180 --> 58:15.060
into planning the problem from a geometric path

58:15.060 --> 58:18.820
to which you add a notion of times

58:18.820 --> 58:20.980
through a notion of time scaling.

58:20.980 --> 58:24.020
And at the same time, you use a differential flatness tool

58:24.020 --> 58:26.700
through its feedback linearization interpretation,

58:26.700 --> 58:30.940
basically that allows you to turn an linear system

58:30.940 --> 58:32.500
into one that looks linear,

58:32.500 --> 58:35.300
to design a trajectory controller for the linear system.

58:35.300 --> 58:38.100
And then what will be the real controller here?

58:40.000 --> 58:45.000
So U is given by this linear feedback copy law.

58:45.300 --> 58:50.300
So what are the corresponding values of A and omega?

58:53.140 --> 58:55.060
I mean, your inputs are not U one and U two.

58:55.060 --> 58:57.660
Your inputs for the unicycle system are A and omega.

58:58.620 --> 59:01.760
What you do is that you compute these two terms,

59:01.760 --> 59:03.540
and then you just invert the matrix J

59:03.540 --> 59:05.140
in order to find the A and omega,

59:06.060 --> 59:09.900
that give rise to perfect tracking

59:09.900 --> 59:11.860
for the Cartesian coordinates, X and Y.

59:11.860 --> 59:16.700
Again, with a caveat that you always have to check

59:16.700 --> 59:18.940
similarity when you invert stuff,

59:18.940 --> 59:20.980
there are ways to deal with the similarity,

59:20.980 --> 59:23.560
more sophisticated ways and less sophisticated ways.

59:23.560 --> 59:26.140
In this class, we'll look at a more naive way,

59:26.140 --> 59:29.580
but still quite effective and also used in practice.

