01:04:18.180 --> 01:04:21.300
But here it is, that if you have the control history of X,

01:04:21.780 --> 01:04:24.100
you can then assume X dot and X of the observation.

01:04:24.100 --> 01:04:25.540
But yes, absolutely.

01:04:25.540 --> 01:04:29.820
You need to also have other measurement of estimation

01:04:29.820 --> 01:04:31.740
of the derivative.

01:04:31.740 --> 01:04:32.580
That's all.

01:04:35.580 --> 01:04:37.100
All right, closed loop control.

01:04:37.100 --> 01:04:38.420
Yeah?

01:04:38.420 --> 01:04:39.260
On the previous slide,

01:04:39.260 --> 01:04:41.180
is it the control gain to be positive?

01:04:41.180 --> 01:04:44.100
Does that just happen to be what makes the eigenvalues?

01:04:44.100 --> 01:04:45.340
Yes, yeah.

01:04:45.340 --> 01:04:47.500
There is something called the Scarf-Sine Rule

01:04:47.500 --> 01:04:50.100
that tells you why this is the case.

01:04:50.100 --> 01:04:52.900
Wiki the Scarf-Sine Rule.

01:04:54.780 --> 01:04:55.740
But yes, now absolutely.

01:04:55.740 --> 01:04:57.180
So there's the positive thing.

01:04:57.180 --> 01:04:59.980
By the way, P here stands for proportional,

01:04:59.980 --> 01:05:03.180
and D here stands for derivative action.

01:05:03.180 --> 01:05:05.300
That's standard notation in control.

01:05:05.300 --> 01:05:09.300
Proportional means that you're multiplying by the variables,

01:05:11.340 --> 01:05:14.980
and the derivative means that you are multiplying

01:05:14.980 --> 01:05:17.980
the derivative of those variables.

01:05:18.860 --> 01:05:20.340
Sometimes there is an additional action

01:05:20.340 --> 01:05:22.740
for two integral actions as well.

01:05:22.740 --> 01:05:23.580
But yeah.

01:05:24.580 --> 01:05:27.660
So here D does not mean desired, it means derivative.

01:05:31.620 --> 01:05:32.740
All right, so closed loop control.

01:05:32.740 --> 01:05:35.060
So as far as two-step design, we're done.

01:05:36.380 --> 01:05:38.620
I just want to give you a little bit of perspective

01:05:38.620 --> 01:05:39.660
about closed loop control.

01:05:39.660 --> 01:05:44.140
So basically, finding control laws that depend on X,

01:05:44.140 --> 01:05:46.420
and they're supposed to work everywhere,

01:05:46.420 --> 01:05:48.940
not just close to your reference trajectory.

01:05:50.540 --> 01:05:53.780
Ideally, this is the holy grail of trajectory planning

01:05:53.780 --> 01:05:55.220
and control and so on.

01:05:55.220 --> 01:05:58.180
Something that tells you from everywhere in the world

01:05:58.180 --> 01:06:00.620
what to do, that's the best you can hope for,

01:06:00.620 --> 01:06:02.540
because basically it tells you that

01:06:02.540 --> 01:06:04.500
even if your model mismatches,

01:06:04.500 --> 01:06:07.340
or dramatic disturbance and so on,

01:06:07.340 --> 01:06:09.940
that brings you far away from your reference trajectory,

01:06:09.940 --> 01:06:13.140
you know exactly what to do in order to achieve your goal.

01:06:13.140 --> 01:06:14.460
Now the problem is that this computation

01:06:14.500 --> 01:06:18.100
is much more difficult to do, to compute as an object.

01:06:18.100 --> 01:06:21.620
That's why we ideally focus on the two-step design

01:06:21.620 --> 01:06:23.460
that represents a good trade-off

01:06:23.460 --> 01:06:25.420
between computation and accuracy.

01:06:25.420 --> 01:06:28.980
But still it's useful to revisit a little bit this concept.

01:06:28.980 --> 01:06:31.460
In particular, there are two main techniques

01:06:31.460 --> 01:06:34.460
to compute closed loop controls.

01:06:34.460 --> 01:06:36.300
These are more complicated techniques.

01:06:36.300 --> 01:06:39.820
Oftentimes they're not computationally scalable and so on,

01:06:39.820 --> 01:06:41.420
but they're out there.

01:06:41.420 --> 01:06:42.820
So the first one is referred to

01:06:42.860 --> 01:06:44.980
as Habito-Yakobi-Bellman equation,

01:06:46.020 --> 01:06:48.860
which basically is a tool

01:06:49.980 --> 01:06:52.540
to compute closed loop control laws

01:06:52.540 --> 01:06:55.700
for general non-linear optimal control problems.

01:06:57.340 --> 01:06:59.220
The problem is that this equation

01:06:59.220 --> 01:07:00.580
is a partial differential equation

01:07:00.580 --> 01:07:03.780
that is very difficult to solve in practice.

01:07:03.780 --> 01:07:06.100
So readily used.

01:07:06.100 --> 01:07:10.220
There is a discrete time analog,

01:07:10.220 --> 01:07:11.820
which is referred to as dynamic programming.

01:07:11.820 --> 01:07:15.700
If you are a computer science graduate student,

01:07:15.700 --> 01:07:18.140
or if you're taking classes in important learning,

01:07:18.140 --> 01:07:21.140
most likely have talked about dynamic programming.

01:07:21.140 --> 01:07:24.580
That's a tool to solve stochastic optimal control problems

01:07:24.580 --> 01:07:25.740
in the general setting.

01:07:26.740 --> 01:07:28.500
We're now going to discuss that in this class.

01:07:28.500 --> 01:07:31.660
Again, two or three in the spring if you're interested.

01:07:31.660 --> 01:07:34.100
This technique really tries to solve

01:07:34.100 --> 01:07:35.220
optimal control problems,

01:07:35.220 --> 01:07:37.100
whereby you not only try to find a trajectory

01:07:37.100 --> 01:07:39.820
that is feasible, but that also satisfies,

01:07:40.140 --> 01:07:42.660
that also optimizes some cost function.

01:07:42.660 --> 01:07:46.300
There is another technique referred to as Lyapunov analysis

01:07:46.300 --> 01:07:48.860
that tries to find a closed loop control law

01:07:48.860 --> 01:07:50.780
that allows you to fulfill a task,

01:07:50.780 --> 01:07:52.660
typically the task of stabilization.

01:07:52.660 --> 01:07:54.540
You want to make sure that your trajectory

01:07:54.540 --> 01:07:56.260
goes to some desired point,

01:07:56.260 --> 01:07:58.100
but you don't have any notion of optimality.

01:07:58.100 --> 01:08:00.460
So if you will, Lyapunov analysis

01:08:00.460 --> 01:08:02.620
addresses more the problem of feasibility.

01:08:02.620 --> 01:08:06.340
Hamilton-Yakobi-Bellman model problem of optimality.

01:08:07.300 --> 01:08:12.300
So this is taught in a nonlinear control class at Stanford.

01:08:12.620 --> 01:08:16.220
I think it's engineering 209, but not exactly sure.

01:08:17.380 --> 01:08:19.140
So what we're going to do in this class

01:08:19.140 --> 01:08:20.460
in the last 10 minutes,

01:08:20.460 --> 01:08:22.940
is just so you look at a specific example

01:08:22.940 --> 01:08:26.380
referred to as posture stabilization or regulation,

01:08:26.380 --> 01:08:29.740
where we're going to derive a closed loop control law

01:08:29.740 --> 01:08:31.180
that gives you some more intuition

01:08:31.180 --> 01:08:33.380
about closed loop control ease in general,

01:08:33.380 --> 01:08:35.500
but it's also useful for the purposes

01:08:35.500 --> 01:08:37.380
of trajectory planning.

01:08:37.380 --> 01:08:39.020
And I'm going to mention that in a second.

01:08:39.020 --> 01:08:40.020
There was a question?

01:08:41.780 --> 01:08:42.940
Yeah, all right.

01:08:42.940 --> 01:08:46.940
So as usual, our problem is a problem

01:08:48.780 --> 01:08:50.940
of controlling a unicycle.

01:08:50.940 --> 01:08:52.580
I mean, it's not that there is only the unicycle

01:08:52.580 --> 01:08:54.100
as an interesting system out there,

01:08:54.100 --> 01:08:56.580
but for this class, I wanted to have

01:08:56.580 --> 01:08:58.900
the same running examples throughout the class

01:08:58.900 --> 01:09:02.220
so that it's easy to appreciate the concepts.

01:09:02.220 --> 01:09:04.420
And also, as I said in lecture one,

01:09:05.100 --> 01:09:07.260
this is a very useful model.

01:09:07.260 --> 01:09:08.900
So the inputs, as you know,

01:09:08.900 --> 01:09:12.260
are the linear driving speed, V,

01:09:12.260 --> 01:09:14.220
and the angular speed, omega.

01:09:14.220 --> 01:09:17.740
The states are x, y, the Cartesian position,

01:09:17.740 --> 01:09:19.060
and the heading, theta.

01:09:20.460 --> 01:09:25.460
So the task is to regulate the posture of this vehicle,

01:09:26.340 --> 01:09:31.340
which basically means from any possible initial condition,

01:09:31.380 --> 01:09:34.140
so any possible x, y, z, theta,

01:09:34.740 --> 01:09:38.100
find a trajectory that brings the vehicle to rest,

01:09:38.100 --> 01:09:40.500
which means that x equals zero, y equals zero,

01:09:40.500 --> 01:09:41.540
theta equals zero.

01:09:41.540 --> 01:09:44.020
So we want to get to the origin of the state space

01:09:44.020 --> 01:09:46.340
from all possible initial conditions.

01:09:48.500 --> 01:09:53.020
So it turns out that in this case,

01:09:53.020 --> 01:09:58.020
it's easier to work with alternate representation

01:09:58.540 --> 01:10:03.540
of the state of the system that leverages polar coordinates.

01:10:04.660 --> 01:10:07.260
So if you don't know what polar coordinates are,

01:10:14.020 --> 01:10:17.020
so if you have a point in the plane, x, y,

01:10:18.060 --> 01:10:20.700
you can uniquely specify the position of this point

01:10:20.700 --> 01:10:23.900
by either specifying the x, y coordinates,

01:10:23.900 --> 01:10:27.560
or the distance of this point from the origin,

01:10:27.560 --> 01:10:29.300
referred to as rho,

01:10:29.300 --> 01:10:32.140
and the angle of the pointing vector

01:10:32.140 --> 01:10:34.900
with respect to the x axis, with respect to theta.

01:10:34.900 --> 01:10:38.260
If I tell you the distance only,

01:10:38.260 --> 01:10:40.420
then the point can be anywhere in the circle.

01:10:40.420 --> 01:10:43.420
Once I also specify the heading theta,

01:10:43.420 --> 01:10:45.960
then I'm uniquely pinpointing where the point is.

01:10:45.960 --> 01:10:47.960
This is referred to as polar coordinates.

01:10:49.220 --> 01:10:51.340
So it turns out that it's more convenient

01:10:51.340 --> 01:10:53.780
to reason about this system in polar coordinates.

01:10:53.780 --> 01:10:58.180
Rho is the distance between the reference point

01:10:58.180 --> 01:11:00.380
on the unit cycle and the origin.

01:11:02.140 --> 01:11:07.060
Delta is the angular coordinate, which I call theta there.

01:11:07.060 --> 01:11:10.120
And alpha is the relative error

01:11:10.120 --> 01:11:13.100
between the current heading and the straight line

01:11:13.100 --> 01:11:15.660
that will lead us to the origin of the coordinate system,

01:11:15.660 --> 01:11:17.260
which is where we want to go to.

01:11:18.900 --> 01:11:22.980
There is a one-to-one mapping between x, y, theta,

01:11:22.980 --> 01:11:25.700
yes, x, y, theta, and rho, alpha, delta,

01:11:25.700 --> 01:11:29.900
which is this distance and these two angles here.

01:11:29.900 --> 01:11:31.740
The specifics are not too important.

01:11:33.100 --> 01:11:34.420
It's not too difficult to derive, though.

01:11:34.420 --> 01:11:37.780
So for example, if x, y is the position,

01:11:37.780 --> 01:11:39.480
this distance is simply the square root

01:11:39.480 --> 01:11:42.940
of x squared plus r squared, y squared, and so on.

01:11:44.580 --> 01:11:46.660
Now, in this alternative representation,

01:11:46.660 --> 01:11:49.140
the dynamic represented by this set of equations,

01:11:49.140 --> 01:11:52.460
again, the specific form is not too important here,

01:11:53.840 --> 01:11:56.780
but I think it's just a little intuition.

01:11:56.780 --> 01:11:59.420
So it makes sense that the distance from the origin

01:11:59.420 --> 01:12:04.420
represented by rho is only affected by the linear speed,

01:12:04.700 --> 01:12:07.960
input, not by the angular input,

01:12:07.960 --> 01:12:12.580
meaning that if I rotate in place with v equals zero

01:12:12.580 --> 01:12:15.620
and omega equals something, I change my heading,

01:12:15.620 --> 01:12:17.960
but I don't get any closer to the point.

01:12:17.960 --> 01:12:19.060
So that makes sense.

01:12:19.060 --> 01:12:22.980
And in particular, the rate at which I get closer

01:12:22.980 --> 01:12:26.440
to the origin depends on the speed at which I'm driving

01:12:26.440 --> 01:12:30.240
and how well the heading is aligned

01:12:30.240 --> 01:12:31.700
with the pointing vector.

01:12:31.700 --> 01:12:33.240
If alpha is equal to zero,

01:12:34.200 --> 01:12:35.900
the rate at which I'm getting closer

01:12:35.900 --> 01:12:37.840
to the origin is maximized.

01:12:37.840 --> 01:12:40.960
If, of course, alpha is equal to pi over two,

01:12:40.960 --> 01:12:42.880
I'm just doing a circle,

01:12:42.880 --> 01:12:47.880
which means that rho dot should be equal to zero.

01:12:49.480 --> 01:12:54.480
Likewise, the relative error ratio to the x-axis

01:12:55.120 --> 01:12:57.660
does not depend on omega,

01:12:57.660 --> 01:13:00.760
while the heading error depends on both v and omega.

01:13:00.760 --> 01:13:03.320
This is just a little bit of intuition.

01:13:03.320 --> 01:13:05.560
Now, the key point, and that's actually

01:13:05.560 --> 01:13:07.900
what I want to communicate here,

01:13:07.900 --> 01:13:12.900
is that we can devise a control law that is closed loop,

01:13:12.920 --> 01:13:15.440
meaning it depends on the state variables,

01:13:15.440 --> 01:13:18.260
rho, alpha, and delta,

01:13:18.260 --> 01:13:20.860
for which you can prove that from every initial condition,

01:13:20.860 --> 01:13:22.360
you get to the origin.

01:13:22.360 --> 01:13:23.580
Now, this looks ugly.

01:13:23.580 --> 01:13:25.980
So you might think, how come someone...

01:13:25.980 --> 01:13:28.900
So you can prove that this is asymptotically stable.

01:13:28.900 --> 01:13:32.760
If you use v and omega chosen in this way,

01:13:32.760 --> 01:13:35.620
and that's what we will do in problem two in your p-sets,

01:13:35.620 --> 01:13:37.640
you can prove that actually your state

01:13:37.640 --> 01:13:38.840
converges to the origin.

01:13:39.700 --> 01:13:41.580
You may think, how come someone could come up

01:13:41.580 --> 01:13:42.920
with something so crazy?

01:13:44.020 --> 01:13:45.980
So usually, and again,

01:13:45.980 --> 01:13:49.940
here I'm just giving you some intuition

01:13:49.940 --> 01:13:51.820
and high-level information if you like it.

01:13:51.820 --> 01:13:52.860
If you don't, no problem.

01:13:52.980 --> 01:13:54.420
Of course, I'm not going to ask you

01:13:54.420 --> 01:13:57.020
to derive this type of control laws in your midterms.

01:13:58.180 --> 01:14:01.160
But just to make the concept consistent.

01:14:01.160 --> 01:14:03.900
So usually the way that this works

01:14:03.900 --> 01:14:06.720
is through Lyapunov analysis,

01:14:06.720 --> 01:14:11.140
whereby you set up an energy function,

01:14:11.140 --> 01:14:13.960
usually referred as v,

01:14:13.960 --> 01:14:18.180
that has the property that is zero at the desired point,

01:14:18.180 --> 01:14:19.320
so at the origin.

01:14:20.320 --> 01:14:23.000
So if you can find a control law that ensures

01:14:23.000 --> 01:14:27.360
that you move along the level set of this Lyapunov function

01:14:27.360 --> 01:14:29.920
in a way that the value function is always...

01:14:29.920 --> 01:14:31.280
So this value function is energy,

01:14:31.280 --> 01:14:32.940
so it's a form of a ball.

01:14:32.940 --> 01:14:35.200
If you can ensure this value function

01:14:35.200 --> 01:14:37.120
is always decreasing,

01:14:37.120 --> 01:14:39.120
then you're guaranteed to go to the origin.

01:14:39.120 --> 01:14:41.380
So then, how do you design a control law?

01:14:41.380 --> 01:14:44.520
You take the derivative of this postulated energy function,

01:14:44.520 --> 01:14:46.000
and then you choose the control law

01:14:46.000 --> 01:14:48.280
in such a way that the derivative is negative.

01:14:48.280 --> 01:14:50.620
And that's, in this case, what it gives you.

01:14:53.040 --> 01:14:55.880
Anyway, so here, this is the point that I want to make.

01:14:56.820 --> 01:14:58.160
This is a closed-loop control law.

01:14:58.160 --> 01:15:00.040
From any possible initial condition,

01:15:00.040 --> 01:15:02.560
it tells you how to go to the origin.

01:15:03.600 --> 01:15:07.000
With a trajectory, you may like it, you may not like it.

01:15:07.000 --> 01:15:09.360
It's feasible, sometimes it does something weird.

01:15:10.880 --> 01:15:12.920
So the reason why I wanted to give this example

01:15:12.920 --> 01:15:15.960
is first of all to showcase what the control law is.

01:15:15.960 --> 01:15:18.980
You see that here, from any initial condition,

01:15:18.980 --> 01:15:20.960
I can get to the origin, this is proven,

01:15:20.960 --> 01:15:24.600
and you will do a numerical experiment in your P-set.

01:15:24.600 --> 01:15:26.840
This is the reference, by the way.

01:15:26.840 --> 01:15:29.760
But second of all, control laws are actually useful

01:15:29.760 --> 01:15:33.240
even in the concept of a two-step design.

01:15:33.240 --> 01:15:34.080
Why so?

01:15:34.080 --> 01:15:36.120
As I said before, in two-step design,

01:15:36.120 --> 01:15:38.200
you first design the open-loop control kernel

01:15:38.200 --> 01:15:40.120
and then the tracking controller.

01:15:40.120 --> 01:15:41.040
So in principle, you're done.

01:15:41.040 --> 01:15:43.600
You don't need to compute a closed-loop control law.

01:15:43.600 --> 01:15:48.380
But oftentimes, what happens is that

01:15:48.380 --> 01:15:50.740
as you're approaching your target,

01:15:51.960 --> 01:15:53.000
so this is a target,

01:15:55.840 --> 01:15:58.280
as you're getting closer to your target,

01:15:58.280 --> 01:16:02.600
you need something that allows you to closed-loop very fast

01:16:02.600 --> 01:16:03.920
and in a very reliable way.

01:16:05.120 --> 01:16:08.200
So this is usually achieved with a closed-loop control law.

01:16:08.200 --> 01:16:10.440
The closed-loop control law, as you can see,

01:16:10.440 --> 01:16:13.240
does not rely on any optimization

01:16:13.760 --> 01:16:15.680
or any solution of algebraic equations.

01:16:15.680 --> 01:16:17.460
Basically, it just tells you that you should be able

01:16:17.460 --> 01:16:22.460
to measure alpha, delta, and rho,

01:16:22.720 --> 01:16:25.160
and then you just compute some sine and cosine,

01:16:25.160 --> 01:16:26.080
and then you have your input.

01:16:26.080 --> 01:16:27.200
So this is super fast.

01:16:28.700 --> 01:16:31.040
So the idea, for example, I have a robot

01:16:31.040 --> 01:16:32.700
that is kind of an international space station

01:16:32.700 --> 01:16:34.740
and has to grasp an object in space.

01:16:34.740 --> 01:16:37.480
So we do the two-step design to get close to the robot,

01:16:37.480 --> 01:16:38.760
but once we're very close,

01:16:38.760 --> 01:16:40.280
which means that we don't have to reason

01:16:40.280 --> 01:16:42.520
about complicated constraints and so on,

01:16:42.520 --> 01:16:45.040
we turn it into a closed-loop control law

01:16:45.040 --> 01:16:47.560
that neglects some constraints,

01:16:47.560 --> 01:16:49.400
but the point is that since you're very close,

01:16:49.400 --> 01:16:51.240
you don't need to consider constraints anyway,

01:16:51.240 --> 01:16:53.760
but allows you to close the loop much faster

01:16:53.760 --> 01:16:55.840
so that you can really do the grasping and manipulation

01:16:55.840 --> 01:16:59.140
as usually requires you to close the loop very quickly.

01:17:00.240 --> 01:17:04.320
And that's actually what you will do in problem set two

01:17:04.320 --> 01:17:06.120
that is due in a week or so.

01:17:06.120 --> 01:17:08.640
We will put all these things together.

01:17:08.640 --> 01:17:11.800
So you will start from the two-step design

01:17:11.840 --> 01:17:14.920
that you're going to develop in your first P set.

01:17:14.920 --> 01:17:18.520
You're going to augment it with a motion planning module

01:17:18.520 --> 01:17:20.940
to deal with state constraints.

01:17:20.940 --> 01:17:23.700
So far, we have not dealt with state constraints.

01:17:23.700 --> 01:17:27.440
On top of it, you will use the posture controller

01:17:27.440 --> 01:17:31.560
to have an additional level of robustness and efficiency

01:17:31.560 --> 01:17:33.440
during the last phases of motion

01:17:33.440 --> 01:17:35.720
in order to get to your desired destination.

01:17:35.720 --> 01:17:37.040
You'll put everything together,

01:17:37.040 --> 01:17:39.920
and then you have an end-to-end trajectory optimization

01:17:39.920 --> 01:17:44.180
module that you can use for your final project.

01:17:45.240 --> 01:17:50.240
Okay, this way of thinking is very much used in practice.

01:17:50.560 --> 01:17:51.760
It's very pedagogical,

01:17:51.760 --> 01:17:55.080
so make sure that you comment your ass with it.

01:17:55.080 --> 01:17:58.040
So what we're going to do on Friday,

01:17:58.040 --> 01:17:59.920
for the graduate students, you should come to the lecture

01:17:59.920 --> 01:18:01.080
or you should watch the lecture.

01:18:01.080 --> 01:18:03.020
For the undergrads, it's optional.

01:18:03.020 --> 01:18:06.120
It's to look at more advanced techniques

01:18:06.120 --> 01:18:09.040
for optimal control and trajectory optimization.

01:18:09.040 --> 01:18:12.420
Specifically, we're going to look at indirect methods.

01:18:13.680 --> 01:18:16.720
All right, see you on Friday for those of you

